														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2380162">
									

<p>Is this a collaboration between you and Metaphysician? If so, Meta can not upvote. If this isn't the case, disregard this. I'm merely asking due to the second paragraph in your post. It's not too clear.</p>
<p>This is certainly interesting with worldbuilding, but the specific type of body horror you go for here is very worn out for me. I can't even be fazed because there's at least three different skips that go for this kind of body horror you go for here on the mainlist, and I just can't care. Maybe I'm tired of body horror in general as well; for it to really grab me in text form, it either has to be vividly described or, barring that (since that kind of thing is near-impossible in a clinical document), go all-out in its effects and imagery to boot (visuals or textual).</p>
<p>The journal at the end was particularly wearisome to go through. It help set up the history of the anomaly and did some more worldbuilding for the Sarkicists, but it wasn't entertaining and it was hard to keep my attention for it.</p>
<p>This is otherwise well-written and executed (even if it's not my thing), and the interviews were good to help make this more interesting. The image is also pretty good, though you should see if it falls under the CC Policy. I just can't get into this like the other articles of its kind, and it didn't elicit a reaction from me other than wanting to get through it.</p>

							</div>

								
					
